n beyond Investor.gov and FINRA; Gemini fallback model use is a production detail with no visible claim.","status":"PASS"}},"findings":[{"category":"sourceSupport","check":"sourceSupport","defect":"The page's central distinction (repeated in the opener, table, How Bond Funds Work, FAQ and takeaway) is attributed to Investor.gov in the table cell, but the saved Investor.gov mutual-funds page says nothing about a maturity date. The saved Investor.gov bond-fund glossary has an empty body, the FINRA bond-funds URL and the SEC bulletin captures are 404/403, and the evidence packet's own unknowns say none of them may be cited. The claim is a reviewer-approved inference from NAV redemption (not the literal Investor.gov text) yet the rendered cell presents it as Investor.gov's statement. Also 'Consequently, most bond funds have no set maturity date' rests on 'a bond fund continually buys and sells debt securities', which no source states.","fix":"FIX table cell Maturity Date, Bond Funds: 'Most bond funds have no set maturity date; shares are bought and sold at the current net asset value (NAV), according to [Investor.gov](...)' -> 'A bond fund holds a changing mix of bonds, so most bond funds have no single maturity date to wait for. Investor.gov says mutual fund shares are redeemed at the next calculated net asset value (NAV).' FIX How Bond Funds Work: 'Because a bond fund continually buys and sells debt securities, the portfolio behaves very differently from a static bond. Consequently, most bond funds have no set maturity date.' -> 'A bond fund holds a changing mix of bonds, so most bond funds have no single maturity date.' Alternatively capture a working primary source (SEC Investor Bulletin on bond funds) and cite it.","id":"A1-1","liabilityBlock":false,"quote":"Most bond funds have no set maturity date; shares are bought and sold at the current net asset value (NAV), according to","severity":"substantive","status":"open"},{"category":"limitationsAndJurisdiction","check":"limitationsAndJurisdiction","defect":"FINRA (cited on the page) defines a bond fund as a mutual fund or an ETF. The redemption-at-NAV description in the opener, table row 'Liquidity and Selling Early', How Bond Funds Work and the FAQ is Investor.gov's description of mutual funds only. The page states it for all bond funds, so a reader choosing a bond ETF is told the wrong exit route (ETF shares trade on an exchange at a market price). The FAQ and takeaway never mention the limitation.","fix":"FIX opener: 'a fund investor holds shares in a pooled portfolio and redeems them at the next calculated net asset value (NAV), minus any redemption fees' -> 'a bond mutual fund investor holds shares in a pooled portfolio and redeems them at the next calculated net asset value (NAV), minus any redemption fees, while bond ETF shares trade on an exchange'. Apply the same 'mutual fund' scope in the table row, How Bond Funds Work and the FAQ answer 'Do you get your money back at maturity with a bond fund?'. Keep the ETF sentence to what the site's index fund guide already says ('An ETF trades on an exchange during the day').","id":"A1-2","liabilityBlock":false,"quote":"a fund investor holds shares in a pooled portfolio and redeems them at the next calculated net asset value (NAV), minus any redemption fees","severity":"substantive","status":"open"},{"category":"sourceSupport","check":"sourceSupport","defect":"Unhedged and broader than the sources. FINRA says bond prices 'tend to fall' when rates rise; no source says every fixed-income instrument falls. The same lead paragraph then says 'Bond funds spread risk across a range of companies and industries', but Investor.gov says mutual funds 'may invest in a range of companies and industries' (a Treasury bond fund holds no companies). The writer self-check flagged this wording (item 9) and the fix was only partly applied.","fix":"FIX opener p2: 'Rising interest rates push bond prices down across the entire fixed-income market, which means both individual bonds and bond funds lose market value when prevailing rates increase.' -> 'FINRA says bond prices tend to fall when interest rates rise, so both individual bonds and bond funds can lose market value when rates increase.' FIX: 'Bond funds spread risk across a range of companies and industries, yet they charge fees and expenses that reduce your returns over time.' -> 'Investor.gov says mutual funds may invest in a range of companies and industries rather than in one specific bond, yet they charge fees and expenses that reduce your returns over time.' Same hedge for the FAQ answer 'Both individual bonds and bond funds lose market value when interest rates rise.' (use 'tend to lose').","id":"A1-3","liabilityBlock":false,"quote":"Rising interest rates push bond prices down across the entire fixed-income market, which means both individual bonds and bond funds lose market value when prevailing rates increase.","severity":"substantive","status":"open"},{"category":"recommendationSupport","check":"recommendationSupport","defect":"An individual bond has no principal protection: the page itself lists credit risk, call risk and price risk on a sale. The takeaway, 'What would change our answer' ('exposing you to severe secondary-market price discounts') and 'your financial outcome depends almost entirely on the borrower's solvency' (How Individual Bonds Work, which also ignores the inflation and call risks named elsewhere) overstate beyond Investor.gov and FINRA. 'Severe' is not in any source.","fix":"FIX takeaway: 'which strips away the principal protection of an individual bond and leaves both options fully exposed to interest rate risk' -> 'which means selling an individual bond at a price that may be below its face value, so both options are exposed to interest rate risk'. FIX 'exposing you to severe secondary-market price discounts' -> 'exposing you to selling at a price that may be below face value'. FIX 'your financial outcome depends almost entirely on the borrower's solvency' -> 'Investor.gov says you receive the face value plus interest unless the issuer defaults or calls the bond.'","id":"A1-4","liabilityBlock":false,"quote":"which strips away the principal protection of an individual bond","severity":"substantive","status":"open"},{"category":"links","check":"linksAndIndexability","defect":"GATE Links, unlinked-company on first mention: 'Investor.gov' (first prose mention in the At a Glance paragraph; the later table link does not cure it), 'Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)', 'U.S. Department of the Treasury', 'TreasuryDirect', and 'Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)' are all plain text. Only FINRA and one later Investor.gov table link are hyperlinked. No render-time auto-linker linked them in the captured HTML.","fix":"FIX: link the first occurrence of each: [Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)](https://www.sec.gov/) notes on [Investor.gov](https://www.investor.gov/); [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/); [TreasuryDirect](https://www.treasurydirect.gov/); [Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)](https://www.fdic.gov/). Define SEC and FDIC before first use (the table cell 'Funds are not FDIC-insured and can lose money' and 'SEC-registered investment company' come before the expansions).","id":"A1-5","liabilityBlock":false,"quote":"The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) notes on Investor.gov that each fund share represents part ownership","severity":"FAIL","status":"open"},{"category":"chatgpt-tells","check":"wholePageCoherence","defect":"monotone-rhythm (measured on paragraph prose, 115 sentences, bands short/medium/long = 4/22/89): sentences 4 to 13 (opener through At a Glance) are ten consecutive 15+ word sentences; sentences 64 to 78 (How Bond Funds Work through Rates) are fifteen consecutive 15+ word sentences; a five-sentence run measures 23/22/24/22/23 words. The 45-word second sentence is an overloaded stack (one idea per sentence rule).